14:50:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The key difference is **when the image has to be created**. ### Video games: render in real time In a game, the graphics card has to generate each frame *while you play*—usually: - 30 frames per second = about **33 ms per frame** - 60 FPS = about **16.7 ms per frame** - 120 FPS = about **8.3 ms per frame** It also does not know exactly what you will do next. You might turn the camera, blow something up, change the lighting, or enter a new area. The GPU must quickly calculate geometry, shadows, reflections, animation, physics-related visual changes, effects, and more for the current viewpoint. That strict time limit forces compromises: lower-detail models at distance, simplified lighting, lower-resolution shadows, approximated reflections, temporal upscaling, etc. ### Shows and animated movies: usually rendered beforehand For an animated film or CGI show, the images are normally rendered **offline**, before anyone watches them. A single frame might take: - seconds, - minutes, - or even hours to render on powerful render farms with hundreds or thousands of machines. Artists can use extremely detailed models, physically accurate lighting, high-quality ray tracing, lots of samples to remove noise, complex hair and cloth simulation, and expensive effects such as smoke or water. Then the finished frames are compressed into a video file. When you watch the show, your graphics card generally is **not rendering the 3D scene**. It is mostly just **decoding and displaying pre-made video frames**, which is much easier. ### A useful comparison A game GPU is like a chef who must prepare a custom meal in 16 milliseconds after you order it. A movie studio is like a kitchen that can spend hours perfecting each plate, then ships the finished meals to viewers. ### Modern overlap Games are increasingly using movie-like techniques—ray tracing, path tracing, advanced global illumination, high-detail characters—but they still need to run interactively. Technologies like DLSS, FSR, frame generation, level-of-detail systems, and baked lighting help make that possible. So it is not that graphics cards “struggle with games but easily render movies.” Rather: - **Games:** the GPU must create a new, interactive image immediately. - **Movies/shows:** the difficult rendering was usually done long ago; your device just plays back the result.
