14:50:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—exactly. Render farms often do use GPUs, and those GPUs absolutely render movie/show frames. The difference is not **whether graphics cards can render them**. It is the available **time and hardware per frame**. For a game at 60 FPS, your GPU has roughly: - **16.7 milliseconds** to produce one complete frame - usually on one consumer GPU - while responding to unpredictable player input For a movie render farm, a single frame can be given: - minutes or hours of computation - many GPUs or CPUs working together - a fixed camera path, fixed animation, fixed lighting, and no player interaction For example, a movie may be 24 FPS. If a frame takes 1 hour to render, that is 24 GPU-hours for one second of finished footage. A two-hour movie has 172,800 frames, so even at an average of only one GPU-hour per frame, it would require 172,800 GPU-hours of work. A render farm spreads that work across lots of machines so it finishes in a practical amount of calendar time. Also, movie rendering can use much more expensive techniques: - Many more ray-tracing samples per pixel, reducing grain/noise - More light bounces for realistic indirect lighting - More detailed geometry, hair, fur, smoke, water, and destruction - Higher-quality motion blur and depth of field - Rendering only the camera views that will appear in the final edit A game GPU could render similarly realistic images too—if you let it spend seconds, minutes, or hours per frame. But then the game would not be playable interactively. So yes: render farms prove GPUs can make those images. They do **not** prove that a single GPU can make them at 30–120 new frames every second while reacting instantly to a player.
