14:51:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes, your graphics card is still involved in putting the show on your screen—but “rendering” means two different things here. ### When watching a show Your PC usually does something like: 1. Reads a compressed video file/stream. 2. Uses dedicated video-decoding hardware—often built into the GPU—to decompress it. 3. Copies/scales/converts the already-made image into a form your monitor can display. 4. Sends that image to the monitor. The hard 3D image creation—the lighting, shadows, reflections, character geometry, etc.—was done earlier by the render farm. Your GPU is therefore **displaying and processing an existing 2D frame**, not calculating what the scene should look like from a 3D world at that moment. ### In a game The game gives the GPU information such as: - where the camera is, - positions and shapes of objects, - textures and materials, - lights, - animations, - particle effects, - shadow data. The GPU must turn that scene description into pixels every frame. If you move the camera one inch, the game needs a newly rendered view immediately. ### Analogy Showing a movie is like printing and hanging up a photograph: the image already exists. Rendering a game is like being handed a 3D model of a room and being asked to paint a realistic new picture from whichever angle someone chooses, dozens of times per second. So your graphics card does help **present** the show image, and it may do scaling, HDR tone mapping, color conversion, subtitles, or post-processing. But it is generally not doing the expensive work of **generating the original image** the way it does in a real-time game.
