14:52:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right: studios absolutely say their hardware—including GPUs in many modern pipelines—**renders the movie**, and that is normal, correct terminology. The important distinction is not a technicality about the word “render.” It is that there are different stages: 1. **Movie production rendering** GPUs/CPUs calculate the final pixels from a 3D scene: models, materials, lights, shadows, reflections, camera lens effects, etc. This is what a studio render farm does. 2. **Playback/display rendering** Your PC decodes a compressed sequence of those already-calculated pixels and displays them. It may also scale them, convert color spaces, apply HDR tone mapping, composite subtitles, and so on. Both involve the graphics hardware producing an image for the screen, but only the first involves solving the full 3D lighting-and-geometry problem. So a more accurate answer to your original question is: > Graphics cards do not inherently struggle to render game images. They can render movie-quality images too. What makes games difficult is that the same GPU must finish each new frame in a few milliseconds, from an unpredictable player-controlled camera, while movie rendering can spend vastly more computation per frame and can be distributed across a render farm. A movie frame might be rendered by a GPU—or several machines—over minutes or hours. Your PC can then play that same frame at 24, 30, or 60 FPS because it is no longer creating the scene from scratch; it is reading the finished result. Also, not every film studio historically used GPUs for the main rendering step. Pixar’s RenderMan pipeline, for example, has traditionally relied heavily on CPU rendering, though modern production pipelines can use both CPUs and GPUs in different ways. That does not change the principle: render farms are doing the expensive image generation ahead of time.
