14:52:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No—that would be an overstatement. Your GPU is doing real work when you watch a movie. It may: - decode H.264, HEVC, AV1, etc. - deinterlace older video - scale the video to your monitor resolution - convert YCbCr video data to RGB display pixels - handle HDR tone mapping and color-space conversion - composite subtitles, browser/UI elements, and overlays - apply video enhancement or post-processing - drive the display output at the correct refresh rate And ultimately it produces the pixel signal that reaches the monitor. That is absolutely meaningful processing. The narrower point is that it usually is **not recomputing the movie’s 3D scene**. It receives something closer to “here are the final pixels for frame 18,432,” rather than “here are the models, lights, materials, camera, and animations—figure out the pixels.” So: - **Watching a movie:** GPU processes, decodes, composes, and outputs an already-rendered image. - **Playing a game:** GPU additionally generates the scene image from 3D data in real time. Neither role is “just a dumb screen.” The game role is simply much more computationally demanding because it must create a fresh view of a simulated scene every frame under a very short deadline.
